# REQUIREMENT 2 — DOCTORS MUST BE ABLE TO VIEW PATIENT MEDICAL RECORDS

## Current problem

Doctors can currently see uploaded prescriptions, but the broader patient medical history is not available through the application.

This is insufficient for a useful consultation workflow.

Audit the existing data model first. Do not assume that prescription files contain the complete medical record.

## 2.1 Establish what a medical record means in the current application

Inventory all existing patient-related information, including:

- Patient profile and demographics.
- Past consultations.
- Consultation dates and outcomes.
- Recorded symptoms or presenting complaints.
- Clinical notes, if implemented.
- Diagnoses or assessments, if implemented.
- Prescriptions and uploaded prescription files.
- Follow-up instructions.
- Relevant attachments.
- Allergies, medical history, or medication history, if supported.
- Appointment and token history.
- Other existing clinical information.

Separate clinical records from operational records.

A booking or token is not itself a completed consultation or a complete medical record.

Do not fabricate missing clinical information or silently interpret an uploaded prescription as a complete clinical history.

## 2.2 Doctor-facing patient history

Implement an authorized patient medical-history view in the doctor dashboard.

When a doctor opens an authorized patient's record, show the available historical clinical information in a clear, chronological timeline.

Where data exists, the timeline should include:

- Consultation date and time.
- Patient ID.
- Treating doctor.
- Clinic and chamber.
- Presenting complaint.
- Clinical notes.
- Recorded diagnosis or assessment.
- Prescription and associated documents.
- Follow-up instructions.
- Consultation status.

Show only the fields that exist and are authorized for the doctor to view.

If a record does not contain clinical notes, diagnoses, or other information, show that the information was not recorded rather than inventing values.

## 2.3 Consultation details

Allow doctors to open an individual historical consultation.

The details screen should display the original consultation information and associated prescriptions or documents.

Ensure:

- The correct patient is shown.
- The correct treating doctor is shown.
- The original clinic and chamber are preserved.
- The original consultation date is preserved.
- Prescriptions are linked to the correct consultation.
- Historical data is not silently rewritten when the doctor's profile or clinic association changes.
- Clinical corrections follow an appropriate authorized workflow and preserve auditability.

If the current application does not support structured consultation notes, document the gap and implement the minimum appropriate data model and UI needed for the supported workflow.

Do not invent a comprehensive electronic health record system unrelated to the current scope.

## 2.4 Doctor access restrictions

A doctor must not automatically gain unrestricted access to every patient in the system merely because they have a doctor account.

Enforce authorization through the authoritative backend or database security rules.

Access should be based on the application's approved clinical and operational model, including applicable patient consent, assigned clinics, legitimate care relationships, and authorized workflows.

Test:

- A doctor viewing an authorized patient's history.
- A doctor attempting to view an unrelated patient's history.
- A doctor attempting to change a patient ID in an API request.
- A doctor accessing a historical prescription through a direct document URL.
- A doctor accessing a record from an unrelated clinic.
- A doctor viewing their own historical consultations across authorized clinics.
- An unauthorized user attempting to access consultation details.

Frontend filtering alone is not authorization.

