# REQUIREMENT 3 — STRICT SEPARATION BETWEEN RECEPTIONIST AND DOCTOR ACCESS

## Core rule

**Receptionists must have access to patient phone numbers for legitimate front-desk operations, but must not have access to patients' medical records. Doctors must have access to authorized medical records, but must not receive unrestricted access to patient contact details.**

Implement this as a backend-enforced, deny-by-default permission model.

Do not rely only on hiding UI elements.

## 3.1 Receptionist permissions

Receptionists should be able to access only the information necessary for front-desk duties.

Expected access:

- Patient ID.
- Patient name for identification.
- Patient phone number.
- Appointment or token information.
- Booking status.
- Check-in status.
- Assigned doctor.
- Relevant clinic and chamber.
- Presenting complaint needed for triage or assignment, where authorized.
- Prescription attachment and approval status only if required by the defined workflow and explicitly permitted.

**Receptionists must not have routine access to clinical medical records**, including:

- Clinical consultation notes.
- Detailed medical history.
- Diagnoses.
- Doctor-authored assessments.
- Clinical treatment plans.
- Historical clinical notes.
- Prescription contents, unless a specific authorized workflow and applicable requirements justify that access.

A prescription upload required for appointment approval must not automatically give the receptionist unrestricted access to the patient's complete medical history.

If the existing booking workflow requires receptionists to verify that an attachment exists, provide the minimum metadata needed for that task without exposing the document's contents.

Where prescription review is genuinely required, document the exception and implement narrowly scoped access rather than silently granting broad permissions.

## 3.2 Doctor permissions

Doctors should be able to access:

- Patient ID.
- The patient identity information required for safe clinical identification.
- Authorized medical history.
- Consultation records.
- Relevant prescriptions and medical documents.
- Relevant presenting complaints.
- Relevant appointment information.
- Clinic and chamber context.
- Other clinical information explicitly supported by the product and authorized by the access model.

Do not expose unrestricted operational, administrative, or account-management capabilities to doctors.

Patient contact details should not be displayed by default unless they are needed for an authorized workflow. If a legitimate use case requires phone-number access, make that access explicit and appropriately controlled.

## 3.3 Administrator permissions

Audit clinic administrator and platform administrator roles separately.

Do not automatically grant administrators unrestricted access to clinical notes or prescriptions.

Separate:

- Operational administration.
- Staff and clinic management.
- Clinical record access.
- Platform-level diagnostics.
- Security audit access.

Any privileged access to sensitive clinical information must be explicitly authorized and auditable.

## 3.4 Implement permissions end to end

Audit:

- Frontend components.
- Backend APIs.
- Data transfer objects.
- Database queries.
- Firestore security rules or equivalent authorization policies.
- Attachment storage rules.
- Direct file URLs.
- Search and export endpoints.
- Cached and locally persisted patient data.
- Historical record retrieval.
- Administrative tools.

Use the existing authorization architecture where possible.

Return only the fields each role needs. Do not send a complete patient record to the receptionist's frontend and merely hide sensitive fields in the UI.

Apply object-level authorization to every patient, consultation, prescription, and attachment request.

## 3.5 Permission matrix

Document and implement a matrix similar to this, adapting it to the application's actual roles and workflow.

| Data category | Patient | Doctor | Receptionist | Clinic admin |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Patient's own Patient ID | Own | Authorized patient context | Assigned clinic workflow | Operationally authorized |
| Patient name | Own | Authorized patient context | Assigned clinic workflow | Operationally authorized |
| Patient phone number | Own | Only when justified by an authorized workflow | Yes, for assigned clinic operations | Only when needed for authorized operations |
| Appointment/token status | Own | Relevant authorized appointments | Assigned clinic operations | Authorized clinic operations |
| Presenting complaint | Own | Authorized clinical context | Minimum needed for triage or assignment | No routine clinical access |
| Clinical notes | Own records as supported | Authorized clinical context | No routine access | No routine access by default |
| Diagnoses and assessments | Own records as supported | Authorized clinical context | No routine access | No routine access by default |
| Prescription contents | Own records | Authorized clinical context | No routine access by default | No routine access by default |
| Uploaded prescription metadata | Own records | Authorized clinical context | Minimum metadata needed for booking workflow | Minimum operational metadata |
| Security audit logs | No general access | No general access | No general access | Explicitly authorized access |

Do not treat this table as permission to expose every category to every role. Implement the least-privilege policy and document justified exceptions.

